.TH DWIM 1 2026-11-20 "dwim 0.3.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
dwim \- a coding agent
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B dwim
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.RI [ PROMPT ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B dwim
is a coding agent that runs in your terminal.
It reads and edits files, runs commands, and works through a task with you in
an interactive shell.
.PP
With no arguments,
.B dwim
starts an interactive session in the current directory.
If
.I PROMPT
is given,
.B dwim
answers it without the shell and exits: the reply goes to standard output and
nothing else does, while progress, the model's thinking, the commands it runs,
and their output go to standard error.
As no one is there to answer the model, it runs in auto mode: a reply
with no command that does not end with
.B DONE
on a line of its own, and the account the model gives after a hundred
tool calls, are answered with word to go on, with the tools on, up to ten
times in a turn.
A reply to a prompt for which the model has run no command, as the answer
to a question is, ends the run as it is.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS The model
.TP
.BI \-\-context " tokens"
Maximum conversation length of each session, in tokens.
Cannot exceed the model's supported context length.
Larger values reserve more memory on the selected device at startup.
.B \-\-help
shows the default on this machine, and
.B dwim
says how many tokens it gives each session, and how much memory the model
and they take, before it loads it.
By default, as many as fit in the memory the device has free besides the
model and 256 MB to spare, shared evenly among the sessions it has room
for, in thousands, and no fewer than 16384:
a GPU's free memory as its driver reports it, which for a GPU that shares
the host's memory is no more than the host has available; on a Mac, as
much as Metal recommends the GPU use, and no more than the host has
available, nor than nine sixteenths of its memory; and on the CPU, the
host's available memory.
Where the device cannot tell, 16384.
A reply's thought leaves the window once the reply is made, as the
model's chat template leaves earlier thoughts out, so that the window
holds the exchanges rather than the thinking behind them.
The conversation is compacted before it outgrows the window: when a
message or a command's output would take it past four fifths, the agent
writes itself a note of the task, what it found, what it did and what is
next, starts over from the system prompt, and reads back the note, your
earlier messages, and the most recent exchanges without their thoughts.
A reply that runs out of room is stopped short and goes on after a
compaction.
.TP
.BI \-\-device " device"
Device to run the model on:
.B gpu
(default), through whichever API opens it, CUDA before Vulkan;
.BR cpu ;
or the GPU through one API:
.BR cuda ,
.BR vulkan ,
or
.BR metal .
.TP
.BI \-\-model " model"
Model to run, by name:
.B inkling2\-mini
(default), which
.B medium
also names; or
.BR inkling2\-small ,
which
.B large
also names.
.B inkling2\-mini
runs at OpenRouter, and on this machine on Metal and on CUDA: its
experts' weights are in NVFP4, the format they were trained in, which
Vulkan does not take. It decodes several tokens at a time with its
drafter, a second file, where the model keeps what is drafted.
.B inkling2\-small
takes more memory than a machine has, and runs only at OpenRouter or
Vercel's AI Gateway.
.TP
.BI \-\-provider " provider"
Where the model runs:
.BR local ,
in the dwim server on this machine,
.BR openrouter ,
at OpenRouter, with the key in
.BR OPENROUTER_API_KEY ,
.BR vercel ,
at Vercel's AI Gateway, with the key in
.BR AI_GATEWAY_API_KEY ,
or
.BR cerebras ,
at Cerebras, with the key in
.BR CEREBRAS_API_KEY ;
where the variable is not set, the key is the one kept from signing in
with
.BR /login .
Without the option, the model runs on this machine where it can, and
where it cannot, at OpenRouter if there is a key to it, or else at
Vercel's AI Gateway if there is a key to that, passing over a provider
that is disabled in
.IR config.json ,
which the model runs at only when the option names it. A session at any
of the hosts
loads no model, has room for 131072 tokens of conversation unless
.B \-\-context
says, and sends the conversation there: what the agent reads of the
project leaves the machine, and each request is charged for.
.TP
.BI \-\-sessions " n"
Most sessions the server has room for at once (default: 2). The weights
are shared, and each session has a sequence of its own on the device, its
key/value caches and recurrent states, allocated when the server starts:
more sessions leave each less context. A session beyond them is refused
until one ends.
.SS Thinking and sampling
.TP
.BI \-\-reasoning\-effort " effort"
How much the model reasons before it replies:
.BR xhigh ,
.BR medium ,
or
.B low
(default).
The model's chat template asks for it with a line at the start of the
system prompt, for
.B xhigh
and
.B low
only.
A host is asked for it with the request, and writes the line itself.
.B low
asks for the briefest thoughts, which the thinking budget cuts short
the least; PrismML, for its ternary build of the model, advise
.B medium
instead, and find that
.B low
thinks about as long as
.BR xhigh .
.TP
.BI \-\-speculate " n"
Tokens the model drafts ahead and verifies in one pass; 0 decodes a token
at a time (default: 4).
.TP
.BI \-\-temperature " t"
Sample at temperature
.I t
instead of the one the model file recommends; 0 always picks the
likeliest token.
.TP
.BI \-\-thinking\-budget " tokens"
Most tokens the model may think at a time before it is made to act, or 0
for no limit (default: 2048).
The model thinks before every reply, and tends to think at length; a
thought that repeats itself is ended whatever the budget. A thought that runs to
the budget is ended with a sentence in the model's own voice, as the
model's makers end one: before the model has called a tool in the turn, that
it will check what it recalls with one; after, that it will go with what it
has worked out from the results rather than go over it again.
A turn ends when the model replies with text alone, or, after a hundred
tool calls without a reply, when it is asked to say what it has done and
what remains.
After twenty calls in a row that change no file, a call's result ends with
a reminder to make the change it has found and check it, or to say what it
still needs to find out.
.SS The session
.TP
.BI \-\-shell " program"
Run the model's shell commands through
.I program
instead of
.BR sh ,
as
.I program
.B \-c
.IR command :
a script that runs them somewhere else, such as in a container that has
what the project needs installed, while the model, and its reading and
editing of files, stay here.
.TP
.B \-\-stats
When answering a prompt given on the command line, print where the time
went once the reply ends: the time loading the model; the tokens read as
prompts, generated while thinking, and generated as the reply, with the
time and rate of each; the tools run; and what a provider charged, where
the model runs at one.
.TP
.B \-\-thinking
Show the model's thinking, and the files it reads, in the shell from the
start, as
.B Ctrl\-O
does.
.TP
.BI \-\-transcript " file"
Write the session's transcript to
.I file
instead of a new file under
.IR ~/.local/state/dwim/projects/ .
.B /dev/null
keeps none.
.SS Instead of a session
.TP
.B \-\-bench
Instead of a session, time the model at several depths of context and
print a table of tokens a second: at each of 1k, 8k, 32k, 64k and 128k
tokens of context, as many of them as the context has room for, the rate
it reads a page of 2048 tokens, and the rate it generates 32 tokens one
at a time. The context is filled with text to each depth in turn, which
takes as long as reading that much, and a row is printed as each depth
is done.
.B \-\-context
bounds the depths timed.
.TP
.B \-\-download
Download the model if it is not yet, print the path of its weights, and
exit. Does nothing but print the path when the model is there, so it can
provision a machine or a container ahead of the first session, and name
the weights for another program.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Display usage information.
.TP
.B \-\-serve
Run the server that has the model, in the foreground, until it has gone
ten minutes without a session. The model takes most of the memory a
machine has, so one process loads it, and every session is a client of
that process over a Unix socket: a session starts the server itself when
none is listening, detached, with its standard error in a log, so this is
for running it by hand or under a service manager.
.BR \-\-model ,
.BR \-\-device ,
.B \-\-context
and
.B \-\-sessions
are the server's: a session that asks for others than the server has gets
the server replaced if it has no sessions, and an error if it has.
Sessions take turns with the model, a reply at a time, each with its own
conversation on the device; a session whose turn it is not waits, and
says so.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.SH INTERACTIVE SHELL
The shell reads one message at a time.
Anything you type is sent to the agent.
While the model is busy, the line above the input box says what it is
doing. The line under the box names the directory the agent works in.
Lines starting with a slash are commands:
.TP
.B /model
Open a picker of the models
and under them the provider that runs them:
up and down move over the models, left and right change the provider,
enter picks, esc cancels.
Picking another model, or another provider, moves the conversation
there.
A model the provider does not run is shown, but cannot be picked.
Only the providers a model can run at are shown, this machine first and
then the others by name: one that is disabled, or has no key, or this
machine without the memory for a model, is left out, and
.B /provider
is where to enable more.
Picking the model the session is on shows it: the device it runs on,
where its weights are kept, and how many tokens of conversation it has
room for.
.TP
.B /provider
Open a list of the providers, this machine and then Cerebras, OpenRouter
and Vercel's AI Gateway, each with a box that is ticked unless the
provider is disabled, and whether a model can run there: ready, or what
it wants, the memory for a model on this machine or a key to a host.
Up and down move over them, space or enter disables one or enables it
again, esc closes the list.
A provider that is disabled is passed over when
.B dwim
starts, and is not shown in
.B /model
until it is enabled again; it stays so in the sessions to come.
A session that is on it when it is disabled stays on it, and a session
with no model yet opens at a provider enabled that is ready.
.TP
.B /login
Open a picker of the providers to sign in to, Cerebras, OpenRouter and
Vercel's AI Gateway: up and down move over them, enter signs in, d signs
out, esc cancels.
Signing in to OpenRouter opens the browser at OpenRouter, which asks
whether to make a key for
.BR dwim ;
where the browser does not open, the address to go to is shown.
Vercel's AI Gateway and Cerebras make their keys in a console of their
own, and signing in to either is pasting a key made there.
The key is kept for the sessions to come.
A session with no model yet opens at the provider signed in to, and one
that is there already opens again with the new key; one elsewhere stays
where it is, for
.B /model
to move.
Signing out removes the key kept; a key in the environment is not
.BR dwim 's
to remove.
.TP
.B /stats
Show where the session's time has gone, as
.B \-\-stats
does.
.TP
.B /transcript
Show where the session's transcript is.
.TP
.B /compact
Compact the conversation now, as happens on its own when it nears the
end of the context window.
.TP
.BI /thinking\-budget " tokens"
Set the most tokens the model may think before it is made to act, as
.B \-\-thinking\-budget
does, with 0 for no limit; alone, show it.
.TP
.BR /auto " on | off"
Turn auto mode on or off; alone, show which it is.
In it, the model goes on by itself as it does when answering a prompt
given on the command line: a reply that does not end with
.B DONE
on a line of its own, and the account it gives after a hundred tool
calls, are answered with word to go on, up to ten times in a turn.
A turn in which the model has made no tool call ends at its reply.
It is off in the shell unless turned on.
.TP
.BR /server " stop | start | restart"
Alone, show the dwim server the model runs in: its process, how many
sessions it has open of how many it has room for, and its log.
.B stop
stops it, to give its memory back, and the next message starts it again;
.B start
starts it now;
.B restart
stops it and starts it again, from the dwim installed now.
The session goes on where it was, with the conversation read back into
the server. A server with other sessions open is not stopped.
.TP
.B /help
List the commands and the keys.
.PP
For work of more than a step or two the model lays out a plan, which the
shell shows over the input box while the model works: the steps, of which
one is under way, and the choices the model made where the request left
them open. Under the step under way is what the model is doing. A step
that is finished goes into the conversation with what it came to, the
files it changed, and the command run since with whether it succeeded;
a step that changed files with nothing run since is marked unchecked.
.PP
A message entered while the model works changes its course: the model is
stopped, and gets the message with the plan as it stands, the steps done
kept. It changes the steps to come to fit, and goes on.
A plan with steps left when the model stops waits for the next message;
.B Esc
then sets it aside.
.PP
The model thinks before it replies, and the shell hides the thought unless
.B Ctrl\-O
shows it: pressed while the model thinks, it prints the thought so far and
what follows, and pressed again it hides the rest.
The same key shows the files the model reads, of which the shell otherwise
prints only the line that says what was read.
.B Ctrl\-C
or
.B Esc
interrupts the agent while it works.
.B Ctrl\-C
or
.B Ctrl\-D
on an empty line exits
.BR dwim ,
printing where the session's transcript is, and
.B Ctrl\-C
on a line with text clears it.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B AGENTS.md
If present in the working directory, its contents are given to the agent as
project instructions.
.TP
.I ~/.cache/huggingface/hub/models\-\-unsloth\-\-Inkling\-2\-Mini\-GGUF/
The model, downloaded on first use from its repository on Hugging Face at a
pinned revision, and kept in the Hugging Face cache as the
.B huggingface_hub
library and the tools built on it keep theirs, so that a copy any of them
downloaded is used, and the copy
.B dwim
downloads is theirs to use. The weights, 17.6 GB, are in
.IR snapshots/<revision>/ ,
each a link to its content in
.IR blobs/ ,
named by its digest; a file still downloading is
.IR blobs/<digest>.incomplete ,
and an interrupted download resumes on the next run. Remove the directory
to free the space;
.B dwim
downloads the model again on the next run. The cache is in the same place
on every system.
.TP
.I ~/.config/dwim/config.json
The configuration, which lists the providers disabled with
.BR /provider ,
by the names
.B \-\-provider
takes, as in
.BR "{ \(dqdisabled_providers\(dq: [\(dqlocal\(dq] }" .
A disabled provider is not where the model runs unless
.B \-\-provider
names it, and cannot be picked in the shell. On macOS the file is in
.IR "~/Library/Application Support/dwim/" .
.TP
.IR ~/.config/dwim/openrouter.key ", " vercel.key ", " cerebras.key
The key to each provider kept from signing in with
.BR /login ,
readable by the user alone, and removed by signing out there. On macOS
they are in
.IR "~/Library/Application Support/dwim/" .
.TP
.I ~/.cache/dwim/states/
The model's state after the system prompt, saved when a prompt is first
read and taken up by later runs with the same model and prompt in place of
reading it again. The prompt changes only with the project's
.BR AGENTS.md ,
since where the agent is, the files there, the branch and the date go with
the first message instead, and the newest four states are kept. On macOS the
directory is
.I ~/Library/Caches/dwim/states/
instead.
.TP
.I ~/.local/state/dwim/projects/
A transcript of each session, unless
.B \-\-transcript
names another file, readable by the user alone. Transcripts are kept in a
folder for each working directory, named by its path with everything but
letters and digits made a dash, such as
.IR \-home\-user\-src\-dwim/ ,
and out of the agent's sight there; each is named by when the session
started (in UTC) and the process ID, such as
.IR 2026-10-01T093012-4242.jsonl .
It is JSON Lines: a record for each thing that happens, each with its
.B type
and the
.B time
in seconds since the session started.
.B start
has the model, the directory, the context, and the system prompt;
.B user
a message, what went ahead of it, and the tokens in the context before it;
.BR thought ,
.BR text ,
and
.B note
what the model thought, replied, and wrote to go on from after a
compaction;
.B reply
the end of a reply, with the calls it made, whether it was stopped short,
the tokens it read, thought, and replied, and the tokens in the context
after it;
.B call
and
.B output
a tool call and what it returned, with the seconds it ran;
.B compacting
and
.B compacted
a compaction, with the tokens before and after; and
.B error
and
.B interrupted
a turn that ended early.
Each record is written as it happens, and a thought, text, or note as it
streams, at least once a second, so a session that is killed leaves its
transcript up to then. Transcripts are not removed. On macOS the
directory is
.I ~/Library/Application Support/dwim/projects/
instead.
.TP
.I $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/dwim-<uid>.sock
The socket the server listens on, in the temporary directory where there
is no runtime directory, as on macOS. A session connects to it, and starts
a server when nothing answers.
.TP
.I ~/.local/state/dwim/server.log
Where a server that a session started writes what it reports: how
loading went, each session opened and closed, and why it exited. On macOS
the file is
.I ~/Library/Application Support/dwim/server.log
instead.
.TP
.I $TMPDIR/dwim-<pid>-<n>/
Where the whole output of a command that printed more than the model is
shown is kept, readable by the user alone, as a file per stream named by the
command's number, such as
.IR 3.stdout .
The result the model sees names the file. The directory is removed when
.B dwim
exits, and any left by an earlier
.B dwim
that is no longer running are removed by the next one to start.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B OPENROUTER_API_KEY
The key to OpenRouter, for a model that runs there, used instead of the
one kept from signing in with
.BR /login .
.TP
.B AI_GATEWAY_API_KEY
The key to Vercel's AI Gateway, for a model that runs there, used
instead of the one kept from signing in. The Vercel dashboard makes one.
.TP
.B CEREBRAS_API_KEY
The key to Cerebras, for a model that runs there, used instead of
the one kept from signing in. The Cerebras cloud console makes one. Its free tier has room for 65536 tokens of
conversation, which
.B \-\-context
says.
.TP
.B HF_HUB_CACHE
Where the Hugging Face cache is, instead of
.IR ~/.cache/huggingface/hub .
.TP
.B HF_HOME
Where Hugging Face keeps its files, instead of
.IR ~/.cache/huggingface ;
the cache is
.I hub
under it unless
.B HF_HUB_CACHE
says otherwise.
.TP
.B HF_HUB_OFFLINE
If set to 1, never download: a model that is not in the cache is an error
that says so.
.TP
.B HF_ENDPOINT
The server to download from, instead of
.IR https://huggingface.co ,
such as a mirror.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
An error occurred.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR claude (1),
.BR codex (1),
.BR opencode (1)
